1
00:00:00,979 --> 00:00:09,640
Welcome to Europe episode four of six. First, I clear the chosen names so we
can study a fresh regional group.

2
00:00:10,303 --> 00:00:26,766
Our seven-place route joins Iberia with Italy and the central Mediterranean.
Andorra, San Marino, Vatican City and Malta use clearly labelled supplemental
maps, because the game omits these small countries.

3
00:00:27,439 --> 00:00:31,653
Let's bring Portugal and its neighbours into a closer view.

4
00:00:34,619 --> 00:00:45,149
Portugal occupies the Atlantic side of the Iberian Peninsula. Spain borders it
on the north and east, while the ocean lies along its western and southern
coasts.

5
00:00:45,803 --> 00:00:55,776
Lisbon’s Tower of Belem commemorates Vasco da Gama’s expedition. The country’s
Atlantic position helps connect that maritime history with the map.

6
00:00:56,434 --> 00:01:04,967
Remember Portugal as Iberia’s western strip, facing the Atlantic. Spain is the
much broader neighbour to its east.

7
00:01:07,792 --> 00:01:19,646
Spain fills most of the Iberian Peninsula, east of Portugal and south of
France across the Pyrenees. The Mediterranean follows its eastern and
southeastern sides.

8
00:01:20,304 --> 00:01:32,506
The Alhambra in Granada preserves the palace-and-garden world of Muslim rulers
in medieval Spain. It is a vivid reminder of the long history of al-Andalus.

9
00:01:33,168 --> 00:01:44,000
Picture Spain as the broad Iberian country: Portugal along the west, France
beyond the northern mountains, and the Mediterranean curling along the east.

10
00:01:45,152 --> 00:01:52,699
Let's locate Andorra on a labelled supplemental map, then zoom in to see its
outline.

11
00:01:58,139 --> 00:02:08,414
Andorra is the tiny landlocked country high in the Pyrenees between Spain and
France. It belongs in the mountain gap, not on either country’s coast.

12
00:02:09,070 --> 00:02:22,584
Its UNESCO-listed Madriu valley preserves mountain pastures, stone paths and
traces of centuries of shared land use. The landscape tells a story of living
with steep terrain.

13
00:02:23,238 --> 00:02:33,768
Think Andorra in the Pyrenees: France above, Spain below, mountain communities
between them. This is a labelled supplemental geographic view.

14
00:02:34,603 --> 00:02:38,655
Let's bring Italy and its neighbours into a closer view.

15
00:02:41,466 --> 00:02:54,643
Italy is the boot-shaped peninsula extending southeast from the Alps into the
Mediterranean. Sicily sits near its toe, while Sardinia lies off its western
side.

16
00:02:55,293 --> 00:03:07,530
The Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed in 1861, and Rome joined it in 1870. A
familiar modern outline came together through a much more recent unification.

17
00:03:08,195 --> 00:03:18,644
Use the boot, Sicily and Sardinia together. Next we will look closely at San
Marino and Vatican City, two separate small states surrounded by Italy.

18
00:03:19,964 --> 00:03:26,489
Let's locate San Marino on a labelled supplemental map, then zoom in to see
its outline.

19
00:03:46,348 --> 00:03:57,052
San Marino is a small inland state entirely surrounded by Italy, near the
Adriatic side of the peninsula. It is separate from the Italian state around
it.

20
00:03:57,706 --> 00:04:08,840
Its historic centre and Mount Titano preserve a republic with roots in the
Middle Ages. The towers on the mountain make a strong image for this tiny
country.

21
00:04:09,496 --> 00:04:19,945
Remember San Marino near Italy’s eastern side: a mountain republic inside the
boot. It is not Vatican City, which sits farther southwest in Rome.

22
00:04:20,914 --> 00:04:28,124
Let's locate Vatican City on a labelled supplemental map, then zoom in to see
its outline.

23
00:04:41,092 --> 00:04:53,074
Vatican City is the tiny independent state within Rome, entirely surrounded by
Italy. We need this close supplemental view to make its outline visible.

24
00:04:53,738 --> 00:05:06,103
St Peter’s Basilica, its square, palaces and gardens form an extraordinary
concentration of architecture inside a very small state. The whole Vatican
City is a UNESCO World Heritage site.

25
00:05:06,762 --> 00:05:18,442
Keep the two enclaves apart: San Marino near the Adriatic, Vatican City within
Rome. Italy surrounds both, but each is a separate country.

26
00:05:19,242 --> 00:05:25,720
Let's locate Malta on a labelled supplemental map, then zoom in to see its
outline.

27
00:05:32,429 --> 00:05:43,133
Malta is an island country south of Sicily, between the central
Mediterranean’s European and North African shores. The main islands include
Malta and Gozo.

28
00:05:43,795 --> 00:05:57,611
Its megalithic temples preserve a prehistoric tradition of building with huge
stones. The UNESCO-listed sites on Malta and Gozo make a striking contrast
with the islands’ small size.

29
00:05:58,270 --> 00:06:09,787
Remember Malta below Sicily: small islands, enormous ancient stones. This
supplemental view completes our Iberian and central Mediterranean group.

30
00:06:14,417 --> 00:06:24,866
Portugal faces the Atlantic along western Iberia; Spain fills most of the
peninsula, with Andorra in the Pyrenees between Spain and France.

31
00:06:25,534 --> 00:06:36,622
Italy gives us the boot, Sicily and Sardinia. Keep its two enclaves separate:
San Marino near the Adriatic, and Vatican City inside Rome.

32
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:52,630
Malta lies below Sicily. Think mountain valley, medieval republic, basilica
and ancient stone temples: four very small states with very different
locations and stories.
